const q=(u,n,prompt,options,answer,why,concept)=>({id:`geo.a.r2.${u}.${n}`,prompt,options,answer,why,concept:'geo.a.river.'+concept});
const make=(slug,title,refs,cards,tasks,practice,check,model)=>({id:'geography.a.rivers.'+slug,topicId:'geography.a.rivers',option:'rivers',title,refs,cards,tasks,practice,check,model,minutes:25});
export const riverLessons2=[
make('erosional-landforms','Waterfalls, gorges and river cliffs',['1.8a','Integrated skill 11: diagram/photo introduction'],[
 ['A river works around the landscape','In an upper-course valley, downcutting and slope processes can leave ridges projecting alternately from opposite sides: interlocking spurs. The river winds around these projections. On the outside of a meander, erosion can undercut a bank to create a steep river cliff. An interlocking spur is a valley-side ridge; a river cliff is an eroded channel bank. Rock resistance and structure affect which ridges persist and how readily banks erode.'],
 ['Follow a retreating waterfall','Where a resistant rock layer overlies less resistant rock, hydraulic action and abrasion can deepen a plunge pool and undercut the lower rock. The resistant layer becomes an unsupported overhang; blocks collapse. Repeated undercutting and collapse move the waterfall upstream, leaving a steep-sided gorge downstream. Geological structure and resistance affect the form; not every waterfall has this exact arrangement.'],
 ['Connect the model to High Force','At High Force on the Tees, resistant dolerite of the Whin Sill forms the lip above sandstone and limestone. Raby Estate describes erosion below the lip, block collapse and upstream retreat. The gorge also records ice-age meltwater activity. Sandy Gerrard’s dated photograph shows the waterfall and rock faces; one photograph cannot measure retreat or prove the complete sequence. The animation is a generalised explanation, not a measured reconstruction.']
],[['Predict: erosion removes rock beneath a resistant lip. The overhang becomes…',['Less supported and liable to collapse','More strongly supported by empty space','A depositional levee'],0],['Compare spur and cliff. Alternating valley-side ridges are…',['Interlocking spurs','Natural levees','Point bars'],0],['After repeated upstream waterfall retreat, a gorge is left…',['Downstream of the current fall','Only above the source','Without any erosion'],0],['What can one High Force photograph establish?',['Visible landform features at that date','An exact annual retreat rate','The complete history without other evidence'],0]],
[q('erosion','p1','Why can a resistant waterfall lip eventually collapse?',['Undercutting removes its support','The rock must dissolve instantly','Deposition always pushes it upwards'],0,'Resistance does not make rock immune to failure when its support is removed.','waterfall-retreat'),q('erosion','p2','A steep bank on the outside of a bend is a…',['River cliff','Point bar','Natural levee by definition'],0,'Lateral erosion attacks the outer bank; deposition commonly occurs inside.','river-cliff'),q('erosion','p3','A river winds between alternating valley-side ridges. These are…',['Interlocking spurs','Oxbow lakes','A row of dams'],0,'Spurs project from opposite sides of a narrow valley.','spurs')],
[q('erosion','c1','Which sequence explains waterfall retreat?',['Undercutting → overhang collapse → upstream retreat','Deposition → lip grows forever → no retreat','Mouth rises → all rocks melt'],0,'Repeated loss of support and collapse moves the fall upstream.','waterfall-retreat'),q('erosion','c2','High Force’s resistant lip is formed by…',['Whin Sill dolerite','Only loose modern beach sand','A concrete sea wall'],0,'The named source identifies the Whin Sill above sedimentary rocks.','high-force'),q('erosion','c3','Which feature is visible in the supplied photograph?',['Water dropping over a steep rock face','A complete abandoned meander loop','A broad sandy beach spit'],0,'Use visible evidence; a single image does not quantify retreat.','photo-evidence'),q('erosion','c4','Which explanation of High Force’s gorge is careful?',['Its history includes ice-age meltwaters and later river processes','One five-second loop gives its real formation time','Only today’s discharge created it today'],0,'The named landscape has inherited history as well as continuing change.','high-force')],'river-falls'),
make('depositional-landforms','Flood plains, levees and point bars',['1.8b'],[
 ['Build a valley floor over time','When water spreads beyond a channel, friction and spreading can reduce its ability to transport sediment. Alluvium is deposited across the flooded land. Repeated deposition, alongside lateral channel migration, helps create a broad, relatively flat flood plain. One flood does not instantly create the whole valley floor.'],
 ['Why the banks can stand higher','As floodwater spills out of a channel, coarser sediment is often deposited near the banks while finer particles travel farther across the flood plain. Repeated floods can build raised natural levees alongside the channel. A natural levee is deposited sediment, not automatically a concrete or earth embankment built by people. Particle sizes and heights in our section are exaggerated.'],
 ['Inside a bend','On the inside of a meander, lower near-bank velocity and transport capacity favour deposition. A point bar, often called a slip-off slope, develops there; the outside bank is commonly eroded. These interacting processes migrate bends and help widen the flood plain. Deposition on one bank can occur while erosion happens on the other.']
],[['Predict: floodwater spreads over land and loses transport capacity. Sediment may…',['Be deposited as alluvium','All change into lava','Make gravity stop'],0],['Compare near-bank and far-floodplain deposits. Coarser grains are often left…',['Closer to the channel','Only at the sea','At identical distances in every flood'],0],['Find the point bar. It develops mainly on the…',['Inside of a bend','Fast eroding outer bank','Top of every waterfall'],0],['A natural levee differs from a built embankment because it…',['Accumulates through sediment deposition','Must be poured concrete','Cannot contain sediment'],0]],
[q('deposits','p1','Repeated overbank deposition contributes to a…',['Flood plain','New igneous intrusion','Glacial arête'],0,'Alluvium accumulates over many events; channel migration also matters.','floodplain'),q('deposits','p2','Why can raised natural levees form next to a channel?',['Coarser sediment settles near banks during repeated floods','Fine particles always travel uphill without water','Every bank is an engineered wall'],0,'Flow loses transport capacity as it spills out; deposition is often sorted.','levees'),q('deposits','p3','Inside-bend deposition forms a…',['Point bar','River cliff','Plunge pool'],0,'A point bar contrasts with the eroding outer bank.','point-bar')],
[q('deposits','c1','A broad flood plain is best explained by…',['Repeated alluvial deposition and lateral channel movement','One moment of evaporation alone','Only a resistant waterfall lip'],0,'Flood plains develop over time through interacting processes.','floodplain'),q('deposits','c2','Compared with the flood plain farther away, natural levee deposits are often…',['Coarser and closer to the channel','Always finer and furthest away','Made only of concrete'],0,'Coarser material commonly settles first near the bank.','levees'),q('deposits','c3','Where would a point bar most likely develop?',['On the inside of a meander','On its eroding outer bank','At every interlocking spur summit'],0,'Lower near-bank flow favours deposition inside the bend.','point-bar'),q('deposits','c4','Which pair can operate together within a bend?',['Outer-bank erosion and inner-bank deposition','Only deposition everywhere','No transport anywhere'],0,'Local flow differences allow both processes in the same reach.','bend-processes')],'river-deposits'),
make('meanders','Meanders and oxbow lakes',['1.8c'],[
 ['One bend, two banks','In a bend, the main fast-flowing thread tends towards the outside bank, promoting erosion and a river cliff. Slower water near the inside favours deposition and a point bar. A corkscrew-like secondary flow helps move sediment across the bed. The complete flow is three-dimensional; our plan view simplifies it.'],
 ['Bends migrate','Outer-bank erosion and inner-bank deposition shift the channel sideways and can enlarge bends. Erosion on approaching outer banks can narrow the neck between two loops. Meanders can also migrate downstream; the same shape does not simply grow forever in one position. Processes depend on bank material, flow and local conditions.'],
 ['A cut-off needs a sequence','During sufficiently high flow, water may cut through a narrow neck and take a shorter route. Deposition then blocks the entrances to the abandoned loop, leaving an oxbow lake. A narrow neck alone is not already an oxbow lake. The lake may later silt up; a cut-off is not guaranteed at every bend or every flood. Our loop replays a possible sequence, not repeated instantaneous creation in nature.']
],[['Predict: deposition builds the inner bank while erosion attacks the outer bank. The bend may…',['Migrate sideways','Remain fixed by definition','Turn into a coastal arch'],0],['At a narrow neck during high flow, a shorter route may form by…',['Cutting through the neck','Growing a new mountain','Stopping every water pathway'],0],['After cut-through, what helps isolate the old loop?',['Deposition sealing its entrances','Only the existence of a narrow neck beforehand','Faster flow through both entrances forever'],0],['Which statement avoids a false guarantee?',['Some suitable bends are cut off during high flow','Every flood creates an oxbow at every bend','All meanders are already isolated lakes'],0]],
[q('bend','p1','The eroding bank is usually on the…',['Outside of a meander','Inside point bar','Centre of the flood plain far from water'],0,'Faster near-bank flow commonly promotes outer-bank erosion.','bend-processes'),q('bend','p2','Which comes before an isolated oxbow lake?',['Neck cut-through followed by entrance deposition','A lake must form before any bend','Only evaporation of the whole river'],0,'A cut-off redirects flow and deposition isolates the old loop.','oxbow'),q('bend','p3','The channel moves laterally mainly because…',['Erosion and deposition act on different banks','Every bank has identical flow','Water cannot carry sediment'],0,'Unequal bank processes shift the active channel.','meander-migration')],
[q('bend','c1','Which arrangement is most typical of a meander?',['River cliff outside; point bar inside','Point bar outside; river cliff inside','Both banks are waterfalls'],0,'Link each feature to local erosion or deposition.','bend-processes'),q('bend','c2','A neck has narrowed, but water still follows the whole loop. It is…',['A meander that has not yet been cut off','Already an isolated oxbow lake','Proof the river is dry'],0,'Narrowing is a precursor, not evidence that isolation has happened.','oxbow'),q('bend','c3','After a shorter channel forms, deposition at the old entrances can…',['Isolate the abandoned loop','Force all water back uphill','Create a resistant dolerite sill'],0,'Sediment plugs separate the former loop from the active river.','oxbow'),q('bend','c4','What is a limitation of a plan-view process loop?',['It compresses time and omits much three-dimensional flow','It provides exact local flood probabilities','It proves every bend follows the same timing'],0,'A conceptual sequence is useful without being a site prediction.','model-evidence')],'river-meanders'),
make('flooding','Land use, runoff and flooding',['1.9a','Integrated skill 12: reading introduction only'],[
 ['Water input meets catchment conditions','Intense or prolonged rainfall, saturated soils, impermeable rock and steep slopes can increase rapid river response. Snowmelt can add water too. A storm hydrograph plots discharge against time; a separate rainfall series helps compare timing. Lag time here is measured between the rainfall peak and the discharge peak. Neither a large rain total nor a steep slope guarantees a flood without context.'],
 ['People alter the response','Urban roofs, roads and drains can reduce infiltration and move water to channels faster. Farming can compact soil, remove vegetation or drain land; effects depend on practice and soil conditions. Industrial development can add impermeable surfaces, drainage and buildings on flood plains. Land use changes both runoff pathways and who or what is exposed. Sustainable drainage and vegetation can modify these effects.'],
 ['Separate hazard from consequences','Flooding can erode banks and deposit sediment, alter habitats, damage crops and infrastructure, interrupt businesses, contaminate water and displace people. Consequences depend on depth, speed, duration, warnings and vulnerability. Our paired hydrographs are invented controlled teaching examples with the same rain: rural peak20m³/s at hour4; urban peak36m³/s at hour2. They illustrate a possible response, not measurements or a universal urban/rural forecast.']
],[['Predict: replace permeable ground with connected roads and drains, all else comparable. Runoff may…',['Reach the channel faster','Always disappear completely','Become unrelated to surfaces'],0],['Read the teaching graph. Rain peaks at hour1; urban discharge peaks at hour2. Lag is…',['1hour','3hours','36hours'],0],['Compacted farm soil can increase flood response by…',['Reducing infiltration and increasing quick runoff','Guaranteeing no rain falls','Changing all rock into chalk'],0],['Industrial buildings on a flood plain can increase…',['Exposure of people and assets','Only river height in every case','Safety regardless of flood depth'],0]],
[q('flood','p1','Same storm: rural peak at hour4, urban peak at hour2. Which peaks earlier?',['The urban example, by2hours','The rural example, by2hours','Both peak together'],0,'Compare discharge peak times; these are invented examples, not a causal field study.','hydrograph'),q('flood','p2','Which land-use change can speed up runoff?',['Connecting impermeable roofs and roads to drains','Retaining all rain indefinitely','Removing every drainage route'],0,'Impermeable surfaces and drainage can shorten travel time.','landuse-runoff'),q('flood','p3','Why can two floods of similar depth have different consequences?',['Exposure, vulnerability, duration and warnings differ','Every community is identical','Depth measures every consequence'],0,'Flood impact is more than the water level alone.','flood-effects')],
[q('flood','c1','A storm falls on already saturated ground. What may follow?',['More quick runoff and a higher river response','Guaranteed unlimited infiltration','The end of gravity'],0,'Limited remaining soil storage can increase rapid runoff.','catchment-response'),q('flood','c2','Using the teaching graph, rainfall peaks at hour1. What is the rural lag?',['3hours','4hours','20hours'],0,'Peak discharge hour4 minus rainfall peak hour1 gives3hours.','hydrograph'),q('flood','c3','Which explains an industrial land-use contribution to risk?',['Impermeable surfaces and more exposed buildings can increase risk','Industry prevents all flooding','Every factory directly creates rainfall'],0,'Distinguish changes in runoff from changes in exposure.','landuse-runoff'),q('flood','c4','Which combines a physical and a human consequence of flooding?',['Bank erosion and business disruption','A road name and a compass bearing','Only the time printed on a clock'],0,'Floods change landforms and can disrupt people and economies.','flood-effects')],'river-floods'),
make('management','Managing rivers and flood risk',['1.9b','Integrated skill 13: diagram introduction'],[
 ['Store water or move it through','A dam and reservoir can store floodwater and release it more gradually if storage and operating rules allow. They cost money, alter habitats and sediment movement, and may inundate land; capacity is finite. Channelisation can straighten, widen, deepen or reinforce a channel to improve conveyance locally, but can alter habitats and transfer a faster flow or increased risk downstream. It does not remove the water from the catchment.'],
 ['Make room and plan land use','Floodplain zoning steers vulnerable development away from higher-risk land: it mainly reduces exposure rather than stopping floods. Washlands temporarily store water on selected land, slowing its release to downstream reaches. They need suitable space, agreements and maintenance; flooding can disrupt farming or recreation. Soft measures can complement engineered structures, rather than being a guarantee on their own.'],
 ['Choose for a place, not a slogan','Compare costs, people and assets protected, habitat effects, land availability, maintenance and consequences elsewhere. A settlement with limited space faces different choices from farmland upstream. Residual risk remains if storage fills, defences are exceeded or equipment fails. Our scenarios show mechanisms without invented benefit percentages or a claim that one method is always best. Use real UK scheme and mapping evidence before recommending a location-specific plan.']
],[['Predict: a reservoir has available storage during a flood. Controlled release may…',['Reduce or delay the downstream peak','Destroy all incoming water','Guarantee no future flood anywhere'],0],['Compare channelisation and storage. A faster straight channel may…',['Transfer water and risk downstream','Automatically store every extra litre','Remove habitat trade-offs'],0],['Zoning keeps new vulnerable buildings off high-risk land. It mainly reduces…',['Exposure','Rainfall at the source','All channel discharge'],0],['What limits a washland’s benefit?',['Available storage, land agreements and event conditions','The assumption its capacity is infinite','A guarantee nobody uses that land'],0]],
[q('manage','p1','Which is a limitation of reservoir flood control?',['Storage can fill and operation must balance different needs','Capacity is infinite','Sediment and habitats are never affected'],0,'Storage and release are conditional, and there are wider trade-offs.','reservoir'),q('manage','p2','What does floodplain zoning chiefly change?',['Where vulnerable development is located','The amount of rainfall in every storm','The rock type beneath every town'],0,'Zoning manages exposure; water can still occupy the flood plain.','zoning'),q('manage','p3','Why assess downstream effects of channelisation?',['Improved local conveyance may transfer a peak or risk','Water ceases to exist at the scheme boundary','Only the immediate banks matter'],0,'A catchment-wide view includes people and habitats elsewhere.','risk-transfer')],
[q('manage','c1','An upstream flood storage area is intended to…',['Hold water temporarily and release it more slowly','Permanently destroy water molecules','Guarantee no maintenance is needed'],0,'Washlands and storage reservoirs attenuate flow within finite capacity.','washlands'),q('manage','c2','Which is a hard-engineering method named in this topic?',['Channelisation','Floodplain zoning alone','A weather forecast alone'],0,'Channelisation physically modifies channel form or banks.','management-comparison'),q('manage','c3','Which is a trade-off of using farmland as washland?',['Downstream protection versus temporary loss of land use','Guaranteed protection with no affected land','Unlimited storage without consent'],0,'Storage uses space and can impose costs on land users.','washlands'),q('manage','c4','A defensible river-management judgement should…',['Compare local benefits, costs and effects elsewhere','Choose the same method for every UK river','Assume defences remove all residual risk'],0,'Evaluate context, alternatives and residual risk rather than a slogan.','management-comparison')],'river-management')
];
